# .env: technical settings and API keys. `make setup` copies this file to .env.
# What the collection IS goes in corpus.toml (make configure), not here.
# Only block 1 is required; everything else has a sensible default.
# Keep comments on their own lines (Docker reads this file too).

# =====================================================================
# 1. REQUIRED: where the language models run
# =====================================================================
# groq   = hosted, fast, cheap, good quality (recommended). Key: https://console.groq.com/keys
# openai = hosted. Also set the three model names in block 2.
# ollama = on your computer: free and private, slower. Needs `ollama pull` of each model.
LLM_PROVIDER=groq
GROQ_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# =====================================================================
# 2. Optional: models (empty = the provider's default)
# =====================================================================
# Defaults for groq:   answers openai/gpt-oss-120b, fast openai/gpt-oss-20b,
#                      vision qwen/qwen3.8-27b
# Defaults for ollama: answers qwen2.5:7b, fast qwen2.5:3b, vision qwen2.5vl:7b
# openai has no defaults: set all three.
# Providers retire models over time: `make models` lists what yours offers now.
#
# LLM_MODEL writes the answers; LLM_FAST_MODEL does small JSON tasks (routing,
# reranking, answer checks); VISION_MODEL reads page images in `make ocr`.
LLM_MODEL=
LLM_FAST_MODEL=
VISION_MODEL=
#
# Any other OpenAI-compatible server (vLLM, LM Studio, Together, a proxy):
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=...
#
# A different provider only for the vision model (e.g. local Ollama for scans):
# VISION_PROVIDER=ollama

# =====================================================================
# 3. Optional: OCR (make ocr)
# =====================================================================
# Pages with a text layer (most digital PDFs) are read directly: exact and free.
# Scanned pages use OCR_ENGINE:
#   tesseract = local and free; install with: brew install tesseract   (default)
#   google    = Google Cloud Vision: best on old or faded scans, paid per page.
#               Run `make install-google-ocr` and set GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS.
#   llm       = the vision model (good on handwriting; cost depends on provider)
OCR_ENGINE=tesseract
# Tesseract languages, e.g. eng+nld+fra (install the matching language packs).
OCR_LANGUAGES=eng
# Pages processed in parallel.
OCR_WORKERS=4
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/path/to/google-key.json

# =====================================================================
# 4. Optional: embeddings (the meaning-based half of search)
# =====================================================================
# local  = on your CPU, no API, no cost; English (default)
# openai = text-embedding-3-small (needs OPENAI_API_KEY)
# ollama = e.g. nomic-embed-text (ollama pull nomic-embed-text)
# For non-English documents use a multilingual model, e.g. with local:
#   EMBED_MODEL=intfloat/multilingual-e5-large
# After changing either, run `make embed` again.
EMBED_PROVIDER=local
EMBED_MODEL=

# =====================================================================
# 5. Optional: database and deployment
# =====================================================================
# Empty = the local database from `make db`. Or a hosted Postgres with pgvector (e.g. Supabase).
DATABASE_URL=
# In production, lock the API to your website's address(es), comma separated.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# =====================================================================
# 6. Optional: evaluation (make eval)
# =====================================================================
# LANGSMITH_API_KEY=...
# EU accounts also need:
# LANGSMITH_ENDPOINT=https://eu.api.smith.langchain.com
